    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a bit too simplified for what i need. the lead time from shenzhen isn't just a flat number, it's got a lot of variance. we have weeks where things arrive fast and then suddenly it's a month because of customs or shipping delays. how do i account for that variability in the formula so we don't run out? maybe something involving standard deviation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To account for lead time variability, you can use the Kerstens algorithm, which incorporates the coefficient of variation (CV) of the lead time into the safety stock calculation. CV measures the variability of a distribution relative to its mean. You can calculate the CV as standard deviation / (2 * mean). Then multiply the demand by the lead time mean and multiply the standard deviation of the lead time by the demand, and use these two values in your calculation.\n\nAlternatively, you can also use the ERPG/ERM (Emergency Reserve Potential and Maximum) method, which is more straightforward but requires some assumptions about the lead time distribution. It's worth noting that these methods can be complex and might require some initial data cleaning and analysis before they can be applied.",
